<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  autounattend.xml - WINDOWS 7 ONLY
  Windows 7 SP1, x86 and x64, from ONE file.
  ============================================================================

  READ THIS FIRST - WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE
    The 8/8.1/10/11 answer file will NOT work on Windows 7. It fails with
    "Windows could not parse or process the unattend answer file" because
    <HideOnlineAccountScreens> does not exist in the Windows 7 OOBE schema,
    and Setup rejects an element it does not recognise inside a component it
    does recognise. Several other settings in that file are also Win 8+ only
    or are pointless on 7. So: two files, one per era. Do not mix them.

  ONE FILE, BOTH ARCHITECTURES - BUT TWO STICKS
    Every component below appears twice, once with
    processorArchitecture="amd64" and once with "x86". Setup ignores whichever
    copy does not match the environment it booted, exactly as on 8/10/11, so
    this single file is correct on both.
    HOWEVER: Windows 7 has no dual-architecture install media. There is no
    "Both" option like the Media Creation Tool gives you - the x86 and x64
    ISOs are separate. So you will have two USB sticks (or one Ventoy stick
    holding both ISOs), and you copy this same file to each one. The file is
    universal; the media is not.

  WHAT IT DOES
    - Accepts the EULA
    - Skips the product key page (see the PRODUCT KEY note below - this
      behaves differently from 8/10/11, read it)
    - Sets locale to en-US and skips the region / keyboard OOBE pages
    - Sets time zone to Eastern (handles EST/EDT automatically)
    - Random computer name
    - Creates local account "User", blank password, in Administrators
    - Stops Windows ever demanding a password change (same 42-day problem
      as Windows 10 - see the PASSWORD EXPIRATION block below)

  WHAT IT DELIBERATELY LEAVES MANUAL
    - Disk / partition selection. No <DiskConfiguration> or <ImageInstall>
      block, so "Where do you want to install Windows?" prompts normally.
      This is intentional: nothing here can wipe a disk unattended.
    - Edition selection, if install.wim has multiple editions.
    - Clicking "Install now" on the first screen (unattend cannot skip this).

  PRODUCT KEY - THIS IS THE ONE REAL DIFFERENCE FROM 8/10/11
    Windows 7 predates the firmware-embedded product key (the ACPI/MSDM
    table). That mechanism arrived with Windows 8. So on Windows 7 there is
    NO key sitting in the BIOS for Setup to find - the empty <Key/> here just
    skips the key page, it does not silently activate anything. You will
    still need to enter the key from the COA sticker (or use the OEM SLP key
    plus certificate if you are doing a proper OEM-channel refurb) before the
    30-day grace period runs out.
    <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI> is the safety net: if the blank key is
    rejected for any reason, Setup shows the key page instead of dying.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY
    - The LabConfig TPM / Secure Boot / RAM / CPU / Storage bypasses. Those
      exist only for the Windows 11 hardware check. Windows 7 Setup has no
      such check, so they were pure noise and are removed.
    - BypassNRO. That is the Windows 11 22H2+ "you must use a Microsoft
      account" workaround. Windows 7 has no online account requirement at
      all, so it is removed.
    - HideOnlineAccountScreens. Windows 8+ only. This is the element that
      makes the other file fail on 7.
    - HideOEMRegistrationScreen. Left out deliberately: retail Windows 7
      media has no OEM registration screen to hide, so it would do nothing,
      and leaving it out removes one more thing that could trip the parser.
    - Set-LocalUser. The PowerShell LocalAccounts module does not exist on
      Windows 7 (PowerShell 2.0), so the password fix uses net accounts and
      wmic, both of which are present on 7.

  HOW TO INSTALL IT ON THE USB
    Copy this file, named exactly autounattend.xml (NOT
    autounattend-win7.xml), to the ROOT of the stick. Do this on both the
    x86 stick and the x64 stick.

  PRACTICAL REFURB NOTE
    Windows 7 install media has no built-in USB 3.0 or NVMe drivers. On any
    machine newer than roughly 2015 Setup will boot and then complain about
    missing drivers, or lose the USB stick partway through. That is a media
    problem, not an answer-file problem - this file cannot fix it. Use a
    USB 2.0 port where one exists, or slipstream the drivers into the image.

  CUSTOMISING
    - Username: change <DisplayName> and <Name> (4 places: amd64 + x86).
      If you rename the account, also change "name='User'" in the Order 2
      command of FirstLogonCommands (2 places: amd64 + x86).
    - Password: put it in <Value> and keep <PlainText>true</PlainText>.
    - Time zone: replace "Eastern Standard Time" (Windows TZ ID, not Olson).
    - Locale: replace en-US throughout.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

    <!-- ==================================================================
         PASS 1: windowsPE - runs inside the Setup boot environment
         ================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="windowsPE">

        <!-- ========================= amd64 ========================= -->
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <SetupUILanguage>
                <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            </SetupUILanguage>
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <UserData>
                <!-- <Key> is REQUIRED even when empty. <WillShowUI>OnError
                     falls back to the normal key page rather than failing the
                     install if the blank key is refused. See the PRODUCT KEY
                     note in the header - Windows 7 has no BIOS key. -->
                <ProductKey>
                    <Key></Key>
                    <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
                </ProductKey>
                <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
            </UserData>
        </component>

        <!-- ========================== x86 ========================== -->
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE" processorArchitecture="x86" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <SetupUILanguage>
                <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            </SetupUILanguage>
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup" processorArchitecture="x86" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <UserData>
                <ProductKey>
                    <Key></Key>
                    <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
                </ProductKey>
                <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
            </UserData>
        </component>

    </settings>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         PASS 2: specialize - runs after the image is applied, before OOBE
         ================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="specialize">

        <!-- ========================= amd64 ========================= -->
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <!-- "*" = let Windows generate a random name. On 7 this comes out
                 as WIN-XXXXXXXXXXX rather than the DESKTOP-XXXXXXX you see
                 on 10/11. -->
            <ComputerName>*</ComputerName>
            <TimeZone>Eastern Standard Time</TimeZone>
        </component>

        <!-- ========================== x86 ========================== -->
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="x86" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <ComputerName>*</ComputerName>
            <TimeZone>Eastern Standard Time</TimeZone>
        </component>

    </settings>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         PASS 3: oobeSystem - the first-boot out-of-box experience
         ================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="oobeSystem">

        <!-- ========================= amd64 ========================= -->
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <!-- Present so OOBE does not ask "Is this the right country?"
                 and "Is this the right keyboard layout?" -->
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <!-- ============================================================
                 PASSWORD EXPIRATION - runs at the first logon of "User"
                 ============================================================
                 Windows 7 has the same 42-day default maximum password age as
                 10 and 11, and an account created by an answer file does NOT
                 get the "password never expires" flag that an account created
                 by hand in OOBE gets. So it would nag, then force, a password
                 change part way through the refurb. These two commands
                 remove that.

                 1. net accounts - clears the machine-wide maximum password
                    age. This is the one that matters, and it also covers any
                    extra account made later.
                 2. wmic         - the per-account "password never expires"
                    flag. On 10/11 this job is done by Set-LocalUser, which
                    does not exist on Windows 7's PowerShell 2.0, so 7 uses
                    wmic, which is present on every Windows 7 install.

                 Nothing here stops the customer changing the password when
                 they feel like it - it only stops Windows demanding it. -->
            <FirstLogonCommands>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <CommandLine>cmd /c net accounts /maxpwage:unlimited</CommandLine>
                    <Description>Remove the maximum password age</Description>
                    <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
                </SynchronousCommand>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>2</Order>
                    <CommandLine>cmd /c wmic useraccount where "name='User'" set PasswordExpires=FALSE</CommandLine>
                    <Description>Password never expires</Description>
                    <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
                </SynchronousCommand>
            </FirstLogonCommands>
            <OOBE>
                <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
                <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
                <NetworkLocation>Home</NetworkLocation>
                <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
            </OOBE>
            <UserAccounts>
                <LocalAccounts>
                    <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
                        <Password>
                            <Value></Value>
                            <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                        </Password>
                        <DisplayName>User</DisplayName>
                        <Group>Administrators</Group>
                        <Name>User</Name>
                    </LocalAccount>
                </LocalAccounts>
            </UserAccounts>
        </component>

        <!-- ========================== x86 ========================== -->
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="x86" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="x86" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <!-- Same password-expiry fix as the amd64 block above. -->
            <FirstLogonCommands>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <CommandLine>cmd /c net accounts /maxpwage:unlimited</CommandLine>
                    <Description>Remove the maximum password age</Description>
                    <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
                </SynchronousCommand>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>2</Order>
                    <CommandLine>cmd /c wmic useraccount where "name='User'" set PasswordExpires=FALSE</CommandLine>
                    <Description>Password never expires</Description>
                    <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
                </SynchronousCommand>
            </FirstLogonCommands>
            <OOBE>
                <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
                <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
                <NetworkLocation>Home</NetworkLocation>
                <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
            </OOBE>
            <UserAccounts>
                <LocalAccounts>
                    <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
                        <Password>
                            <Value></Value>
                            <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                        </Password>
                        <DisplayName>User</DisplayName>
                        <Group>Administrators</Group>
                        <Name>User</Name>
                    </LocalAccount>
                </LocalAccounts>
            </UserAccounts>
        </component>

    </settings>

</unattend>
